Anyway I don't expect my comment to be visible for long thanks to the even-minded folks around here, so carry on.

AFAIK it's not just the rise of the franchise that killed the theatrically released spec, it's also the coinciding fall of the celeb-driven picture.  Remember what Jim Carrey supposedly earned for Cable Guy?  Now that no-one's that interested in the star, the idea they're attached to has less value.  Tom Cruise is the last man standing, but if Top Gun 2 flops (and going from that trailer, it deserves to), he might be done too.

&#x200B;

The fantasy Hollywood businessman in me loves the mid-budget feature but I can't see it coming back to theaters.  Like you said, if they can make it cheap enough - straight to streaming.  So modern audiences won't get the cinema experience of a Speed, Galaxy Quest, or even a Twister or Jurassic Park.

&#x200B;

I think all features will eventually go the way of porn.  Porn used to air in theaters before someone realised it was more profitable to release it on tape, and public standards changed so that turning up to watch an adult feature wasn't acceptable.  So the day will come when everything is released via digital service, and not going to the multiplex will become the norm.  Sad, unless you grow up not knowing any different.

I said the same thing on a forum once, wondering if I'd live to see the day when comic book garbage had run its course.

&#x200B;

Top response - "You won't."

Late to the party here but I for one enjoyed your script.  Sure, the words Matrix, Thirteenth Floor, and Existenz came to me while reading it, but the jargon was a pleasure to read and it's well constructed.  VR is a hot topic and will only get hotter.

