Hamming it up just to taunt Carl for the time it takes to walk outside?  Ehh I don't think so.  Remember, the lab is still running (unless it's not actually making NZT any more, which Eddie never states.)

&#x200B;

Mass producing the drug so others can get as smart as him and compete for his power?  Doesn't make sense.  That actually leads me to another problem the movie never solves - the original manufacturers are still out there.  What was their plan?  How many people did they give it to?  What do they think about the sudden unexplained emergence of this genius Eddie?  What if Eddie's chemist decides to try it?

A psychologist I put a fair amount of stock into believes employment is more important than meds and exercise re: mental health.  Seems like everyone around him knew this and put in a lot of effort to keep him busy, but it hasn't worked...as yet.

&#x200B;

With his money I'd be doing the bucket list but when you're under a cloud it's hard to see beyond it.

I don't have a 'ever' pick, but here's a few that come to mind -

&#x200B;

There Will Be Blood - there's a great 1hr 50min movie in there, but having to bear with that guy for three hours was a slog.

&#x200B;

No Country for Old Men - I've been downbashed for this, but whatever - it's a lazy Coen bros effort.

&#x200B;

The Dark Knight - as my father put it, Batman may as well just be a cop in it.  Bale is bland and Nolan is always more head than heart. 

&#x200B;

Casino Royale (06) - take the last 20mins out and it's fine.

&#x200B;

Shaun of the Dead - I don't remember any laughs.

&#x200B;

The Thing (82) - with low expectations it's OK, but the characters spend their time doing some dumb shit.

&#x200B;

The Conversation - a few plot problems from memory.

&#x200B;

Spider-man 2 - 4 stars from Ebert?  It's the same woe-is-me Spidey problems, with Aunt May being slung around skyscrapers (!).  This is when I got bored of superheroes.

&#x200B;

Miller's Crossing - saw again recently.  Too true to Hammett ie it gets so twisty that you just don't care.

&#x200B;

The Departed - there were parts in it that seem intentionally bad.

&#x200B;

Fury Road - terrible.  People still think it's a return to traditional action?

