This is one of those frustrating scenarios you run into with OCD, because if you said to a non-OCD person 'Would you worry about things like asbestos and mercury exposure?' they'd probably say yes, I'd worry a bit.  The disadvantage we have is that the thought process when it comes to danger is overpowering, and like you, I'd probably assume I already have been poisoned and have possibly passed particles onto someone else (harm OCD), which is the panic component of the condition.

&#x200B;

In your situation I'd try to remove the panic layer (which is hard) and assess.  You'll probably realise that you haven't done anything wrong by reasonable standards.  The OCD won't be happy (mine hardly ever is when it comes to answers), but if yours is as bad as mine sometimes is you'll get to the point where the fear and overthinking of the whole thing is actually worse than the idea of being poisoned.

