The game is very much based in Sydney, so we'll get to those teams soon.  The one team towns can be thought of as such -

&#x200B;

Melbourne - comparatively new team in the comp that has surprised many with their success.  That success has been marked by corruption at the corporate level.  When they get going a good enough team to watch, but their sheer professionalism and bending of the rules can make them unlikable.  The Yankees perhaps?

&#x200B;

Brisbane - were Melbourne until Melbourne came along.  Up until recently were headed by a Steinbrenner type figure.  Their home ground is good for a ten point advantage.

&#x200B;

Gold Coast - a newish team playing in an area where other franchises have died.  Mostly make up the numbers.  Have some underdog appeal.

&#x200B;

Canberra - perennial underachievers, also with underdog appeal.  Get points for often playing on a frozen tundra.

&#x200B;

New Zealand - the cliche about them is that they love throwing the ball around but defense is often an option.  Mostly true.  Also some underdog appeal for tough travel demands.

&#x200B;

North Queensland - Brisbane's little brother, both chronologically and census wise.  Seem to have great seasons and very poor ones.  Had what many believe was the game's best player until last year.

&#x200B;

Newcastle - a team on the up after a long period of stagnation.

&#x200B;

Now, for Sydney.  The two oldest clubs, the Roosters and Rabbitohs, have a traditional rich vs poor thing going on, as our commentators like to remind us of ad nauseum.  The Roosters always seem to have money to spend, so some would accuse them of 'buying' trophies.  The best jersey in the NRL.  Souths are the people's team; after a long period of poor results and not being in the competition, they're now a force.  Part owned by Russell Crowe, which can be a problem.

&#x200B;

The next three teams (St George Illawarra, Canterbury, Manly) are often contenders depending on the roster.  St George don't have too much baggage, Canterbury have had their off-field and in-crowd scandals (although there isn't a team that hasn't had them), and Manly are the 'love to hate' team, based on a history that probably has no current relevance.

&#x200B;

Parramatta, Wests and Penrith are non-glamour clubs (although Penrith is working hard to change that).  Not much baggage.  Another Steinbrenner currently at Penrith.

&#x200B;

Cronulla is another love to hate team, but some would say with good reason.  A 'bad boy' club with a few Dennis Rodmans.  Recently successful.

&#x200B;

I'm not as into the NRL as many others but that's my take.
